---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Wordmark from "../components/Wordmark.astro";
import { ctfNames, figures } from "../data/site";

/* The hierarchy is stated as data so the marks, the order and the wording of
   each rung cannot drift apart. Roles are the accurate ones: Pixora is FCOM's
   official club and UCYSS sits inside it. */
const hierarchy = [
	{
		name: "Universiti Poly-Tech Malaysia",
		role: "The university. Cyberjaya, Selangor.",
		logo: { src: "/logos/uptm.png", width: 320, height: 143, box: 64 },
		indent: "sm:pl-0",
		alt: "Universiti Poly-Tech Malaysia",
	},
	{
		name: "Faculty of Computing & Multimedia",
		role: "FCOM, UPTM. One faculty club: Pixora.",
		logo: { src: "/logos/fcom.png", width: 320, height: 125, box: 64 },
		indent: "sm:pl-8",
		alt: "Faculty of Computing and Multimedia, UPTM",
	},
	{
		name: "Pixora Society Club",
		role: "The official FCOM club. Broad computing community — events, welfare, competitions.",
		logo: { src: "/logos/pixora.png", width: 320, height: 320, box: 46 },
		indent: "sm:pl-16",
		alt: "Pixora Society Club, FCOM",
	},
	{
		name: "UCYSS",
		role: "A focus group inside Pixora. Weekly sessions taught by members, beginner-first, and a CTF team.",
		logo: null,
		indent: "sm:pl-24",
		alt: "",
	},
];

const next = [
	{ href: "/what-we-do", label: "What we do", note: "The three things, in detail." },
	{ href: "/join", label: "Join UCYSS", note: "How membership actually works." },
	{ href: "/writeups", label: "Writeups", note: "Everything we have published so far." },
];
---

<Base
	title="About"
	description="How UCYSS works: weekly sessions taught by members, beginner-first, plus a CTF team that competes together. And where UCYSS sits inside Pixora, FCOM and UPTM."
>
	<!-- ==================== MASTHEAD ==================== -->
	<section class="border-b border-line">
		<div
			class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-5 py-16 md:px-8 md:py-24 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-10"
		>
			<div class="lg:col-span-8">
				<p class="label-mono text-accent">About</p>
				<!-- {" "} keeps a real space where the line breaks — Astro collapses the
				     whitespace before the span, and "knowingalmost" is not a word. -->
				<h1 class="mt-7 max-w-2xl text-display font-light">
					Most of us started knowing{" "}
					<span class="font-semibold">almost nothing</span>.
				</h1>
			</div>

			<div class="hidden lg:col-span-4 lg:flex lg:justify-end lg:pt-2">
				<Wordmark size="lg" alt="" />
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- ==================== BODY ==================== -->
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 py-16 md:px-8 md:py-20">
		<div class="prose">
			<p class="text-lede text-muted">
				Three names get used loosely and it causes real confusion, so it is worth being
				precise about which is which before anything else.
			</p>

			<h2>Where UCYSS sits</h2>

			<p>
				<strong>Pixora</strong> is the official student club of the Faculty of Computing
				&amp; Multimedia at Universiti Poly-Tech Malaysia. It is a broad computing
				community — events, welfare, competitions, the ordinary business of a faculty
				club. It predates us, and it would carry on perfectly well without us.
			</p>

			<p>
				<strong>UCYSS</strong> is a focus group inside Pixora. We are not a separate
				society, we are not a rival to Pixora, and we are not competing for the same
				recognition. What we took from that remit is a weekly habit: one member teaches
				something, the rest of the room learns it, and a team of us enters CTFs
				together. There are {figures.members.value} members.
			</p>

			<!-- The hierarchy, as a ladder rather than a box diagram. Each rung is
			     one institution, one supplied mark, and its real role. -->
			<ol class="mt-10 border-t border-line">
				{
					hierarchy.map((row, i) => {
						const last = i === hierarchy.length - 1;
						return (
							<li class:list={["flex items-stretch gap-4 border-b border-line py-5 sm:gap-6", row.indent]}>
								<span class="relative w-4 shrink-0 self-stretch sm:w-5" aria-hidden="true">
									<span
										class:list={[
											"absolute left-0 w-px bg-line-strong",
											last ? "top-0 h-1/2" : "inset-y-0",
										]}
									/>
									<span class="absolute left-0 top-1/2 h-px w-3.5 bg-line-strong" />
								</span>

								{row.logo ? (
									<img
										src={row.logo.src}
										alt={row.alt}
										width={row.logo.width}
										height={row.logo.height}
										loading="lazy"
										decoding="async"
										style={`width:${row.logo.box}px;height:auto`}
										class="my-0.5 shrink-0 self-center opacity-60"
									/>
								) : (
									<span class="my-0.5 flex shrink-0 items-center self-center">
										<Wordmark />
									</span>
								)}

								<span class="min-w-0">
									<span class="block text-[1.0625rem] font-semibold tracking-tight text-text">
										{row.name}
									</span>
									<span class="mt-1.5 block text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-muted">
										{row.role}
									</span>
								</span>
							</li>
						);
					})
				}
			</ol>

			<div class="callout my-10">
				<p class="m-0">
					Most of what UCYSS does runs through Pixora's programmes. The Hack The Box lab
					ran on Pixora's protocol, technical support, media and food teams. The CyberDSA
					2026 trip was submitted by the Pixora treasurer against the faculty's ATAP
					allocation, and we travelled as part of the Pixora delegation. There is no
					separate UCYSS budget line to fight over, which is a feature.
				</p>
			</div>

			<blockquote class="my-12">
				<p class="m-0 text-[1.375rem] leading-snug font-light tracking-tight text-text md:text-2xl">
					“The week you know something is the week you teach it.”
				</p>
			</blockquote>

			<h2>How a week works</h2>

			<p>
				We usually meet on a Wednesday, and a meeting has one shape to it. A member
				volunteers to teach something they learned recently — something that interested
				them, or something they had to work out for themselves. Everyone else comes to
				learn it. There is no syllabus and no guest speaker. The person at the front
				learned it a few weeks ago.
			</p>

			<p>
				Not every week gets a session. When nobody has volunteered, or it is finals, or
				everyone is busy, we skip it. Wednesday is the habit, not a guarantee.
			</p>

			<p>
				That is why the sessions assume you start from nothing. If you have never opened a
				terminal, the Linux session is a perfectly reasonable first one to walk into.
				Nobody is put on the spot, and nobody is expected to keep up with the member who
				has been at this for a year. The topic changes every week on purpose, so no one
				can fall behind on all of it. OSINT, web exploitation with Burp Suite, and Linux
				for beginners are three of the ones we have run.
			</p>

			<p>
				Being a beginner here is not a gap to apologise for. Everyone in the room was new
				at some point, most people are new now, and the people who have been around
				longest remember being exactly where you are.
			</p>

			<h2>The CTF team</h2>

			<p>
				UCYSS has its own team, and we enter competitions as a team rather than as a
				scattering of individuals. So far: {ctfNames.join(", ")}. SunCTF is backed by the
				university, which covers transport; the rest the team raises itself.
			</p>

			<p>
				Afterwards, if the team asks for it, we debrief. Someone puts up a flag they found
				and the group works through how it was actually got: the tool, the step, the hour
				lost down a dead end. Nobody writes it up as a report. It is a conversation, and
				everybody on the team hears it.
			</p>

			<h2>Joining</h2>

			<p>
				Membership runs through Pixora. You join Pixora, you tell them you want the
				security track, and you turn up. There is no application test, no interview, and
				no prerequisite module. If you can open a terminal and are willing to read a
				walkthrough, you can keep up.
			</p>

			<p>
				Meetings are open to any FCOM student. Most people arrive knowing less than they
				think they do, which is the correct amount to know.
			</p>

			<h2>What we publish</h2>

			<p>
				Anything worth keeping goes up as a writeup, and the archive is a record of what
				the community has actually done. The notes are the members' own — written by
				whoever ran the session, in the state they got it working, and left that way on
				purpose. Roughly {figures.sessionsRun.value} sessions and activities are logged
				so far. A half-finished note is still useful to whoever reads it next year.
			</p>

			<p>
				One habit we do keep: nothing about anyone else goes in. Names, IC numbers,
				matric numbers, emails and phone numbers belong to people who never agreed to be
				in a writeup, so they stay out. It is the least interesting paragraph on this
				page and the one we care about most.
			</p>

			<div class="callout my-10">
				<p class="m-0">
					One of the OSINT slides was a real 2019 group-project report, complete with
					everyone's IC and matric numbers. It is the reason this site has a privacy
					notice, and the reason that rule exists.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<!-- ==================== WHERE NEXT ==================== -->
		<nav aria-label="About UCYSS" class="mt-20 border-t border-line pt-10 md:mt-24">
			<h2 class="label-mono text-faint">Where next</h2>
			<ul class="mt-6 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-1 sm:grid-cols-3">
				{
					next.map((item) => (
						<li class="border-b border-line">
							<a
								href={item.href}
								class="group flex min-h-16 flex-col justify-center py-3 transition-colors duration-200"
							>
								<span class="text-[1.0625rem] font-medium tracking-tight text-text transition-colors duration-200 group-hover:text-accent">
									{item.label}
								</span>
								<span class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">{item.note}</span>
							</a>
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ul>
		</nav>
	</div>
</Base>
